{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "108881ae9e4b7229dc2db493a51d2fc7a4f25784abe5dc55556d2ebbf8f385ee"} {"question" "q-15384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "12\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "13\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "14\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "15\\,\\text{min}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15384" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\,\\text{min}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "108881ae9e4b7229dc2db493a51d2fc7a4f25784abe5dc55556d2ebbf8f385ee"} {"question" "q-15595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474a