ings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "108881ae9e4b7229dc2db493a51d2fc7a4f25784abe5dc55556d2ebbf8f385ee"} {"question" "q-106936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(30-T)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+30)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-30)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(T-30)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "108881ae9e4b7229dc2db493a51d2fc7a4f25784abe5dc55556d2ebbf8f385ee"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-450k(T-75)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-75k(T-450)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+75)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-450)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-106937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "108881ae9e4b7229dc2db493a51d2fc7a4f25784abe5dc55556d2ebbf8f385ee"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "13\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "15\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "8\\,\\text{min}"] ["math" "9\\,\\text{min}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-107122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9\\,\\text{min}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/state.json" "file_sha256" "67b4c4d78355bc6c4c4559e1f2703de5e813d430e2fd09822daf409e9474acf3"} {"question" "q-15595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "3eefc423b2a74688327b0ba3c49a2ab2206ea976dcff841c95f401fe6842a514" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "Solving T'=−0.05(T−25) with T(0)=100 gives T(t)=25+75e^(−0.05t). Thus T(6)=25+75e^(−0.3)≈80.56137°C, rounding to 81°C. Original option c (index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option a, 83°C, was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. The stored degree notation and captured degree glyph denote the same Celsius quantity." "bindings" {"problem" "610ff75da4559785a655946a7d994871c4c153c7ac49bc1fb46a36199e08aa9c" "worked_solution" "ee9ccbc40389bb12f430be842755fd38fdb6f4be0aa56e1d5473fb65732f0645" "original_field" "c577fee98e7bba7a496faac37ebcb1f77e2196083544b4fc00f384a47960a0d5" "reviewed_field" "cf57609baead66a26915e3b487355fbdee0fcbfa259c9e5ae8d0abec02eea021" "raw_widget" "b612cf57f2a9ebc6714caabb4a8bbac0f2f9d34514f3d9da2d0380bafe440211"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-15595-before.json" "sha256" "9ab2ac115c63b18e7aa38ceb0d02b6deee559270a22c0022c276bc5180a7cdfd"} {"path" "q-15595-before.png" "sha256" "9467239b2c8b69a4010abbafd41d9e5f2a649ea8b293ba8314120e03d93e1346"} {"path" "q-15595-after.png" "sha256" "bdc31d30a35be352dc63e23b8bb10ec27c670e21117e8e7e09442c8ea6b1fbd2"} {"path" "history-q-15595.json" "sha256" "263fa3a9ccc893e85b9df12324fa1ad5172e811474ad8deb85abf964d80cea91"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "b5470d74c6260d7af9076740faee8905cd7916f128bd9703619d82a3f1ec722f"}]} {"question" "q-15595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}"] ["math" "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066127/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "3eefc423b2a74688327b0ba3c49a2ab2206ea976dcff841c95f401fe6842a514" "rationale" "Solving T'=−0.05(T−25) with T(0)=100 gives T(t)=25+75e^(−0.05t). Thus T(6)=25+75e^(−0.3)≈80.56137°C, rounding to 81°C. Original option c (index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option a, 83°C, was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. The stored degree notation and captured degree glyph denote the same Celsius quantity."}] "usage" {"q-15384" [] "q-15595" []} "basis" 2177 "retractions" [[17592186078662 :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of $50\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. The thermometer is then transferred to a $10\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is $40\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$. To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of $30\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t > 0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the $10\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$ water bath.*"] [17592186078662 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law gives $T(t)=10+Ce^{-kt}$. From $T(0)=50$, $C=40$. Then $T(5)=40$ gives $e^{-5k}=30/40=3/4$, so $k=\\ln(4/3)/5$. To reach $30\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, solve $30=10+40e^{-kt}$, hence $e^{-kt}=1/2$. Thus\n\n$$t=\\frac{5\\ln 2}{\\ln(4/3)}\\approx12.047\\text{ minutes}.$$\n\nThe total time since transfer is $12$ minutes to the nearest minute."] [17592186078663 :question/problem "The temperature $T(t)$, in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dT}{dt} =-0.05(T - 25)$\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is $100\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$, calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer."] [17592186078663 :question/worked-solution "Separating variables gives $\\ln|T-25|=-0.05t+C$, so $T(t)=25+Ae^{-0.05t}$. The initial condition $T(0)=100$ gives $A=75$. Therefore\n\n$$T(6)=25+75e^{-0.3}\\approx80.56\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C},$$\n\nwhich rounds to $81\\,^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}$."] [17592186078663 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186078663 :question/answer-fields 17592186075287]] "immutable_answers" [17592186075282 17592186075283 17592186075284 17592186075285 17592186075286 17592186075288 17592186075289 17592186075290 17592186075291 17592186075292] "immutable_fields" [[17592186075281 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075281 :answer-field/id] [17592186075281 :answer-field/key] [17592186075281 :answer-field/type] [17592186075287 :answer-field/correct] [17592186075287 :answer-field/id] [17592186075287 :answer-field/key] [17592186075287 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
